    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nWhen saving files, neovim appears to be changing the `endofline` setting to `noendofline`. The `[noeol]` warning appears below the statusline and this causes git to see the final line of the file as changed (even if it wasn't).\n\nIf I type `:set eol` to set it explicitly then re-write the file, the EOL is restored.\n\nThis behaviour has been experienced on `.lua` and `.md` files which made me think it was treesitter related, but also happens on `.txt` files so probably not due to that.\n\nI have been rebuilding my nvim configs so this was a clean state with no plugins loaded.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n``` shell\necho 'foo' > /tmp/foo.txt\nnvim --clean /tmp/foo.txt\n```\n``` vim\n:set eol?\nendoffile\n\n:w\n\"foo.txt\" [noeol] 1L,3B written\n\n:set eol?\nnoendofline\n\n:set eol\n:w\n\"foo.txt\" [noeol] 1L,4B written\n```\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nThe default behaviour on linux is to write the EOL character and this should not be changed within an editing session, unless the user explicitly chooses to.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nv0.13.0-dev-813+ge715122e4e\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nNo - tested with Vim 9.2.0478\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nFedora 44\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nalacritty and kitty\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nalacritty and xterm-kitty respectively\n\n### Installation\n\nsystem package manager (v0.12.2) and bob (v0.13.0)",
